WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 65: The Great Mammoth Cave Entombment of Floyd Collins

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:17.280
[Rebecca] In the winter of 1925, deep beneath the rolling limestone hills of central Kentucky, an ordinary farm boy and master caver crawled into a narrow, uncharted crevice.

4
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.400
[Rebecca] When a falling boulder pinned his left foot, Floyd Collins became the center of the world.

5
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:32.880
[Rebecca] Over 18 freezing days, his plight captured the imagination of a nation, giving birth to the first modern multimedia-breaking news sensation in American history.

6
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:41.680
[Rebecca] Radio broadcasts, telegraph wires, and carnival crowds surrounded Sand Cave in an extraordinary spectacle of hope and heartbreak.

7
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.680
[Rebecca] George brings us the harrowing story of Floyd Collins.

8
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:55.200
[George] In the 1920s, central Kentucky was engulfed in an intense commercial struggle known as the Kentucky Cave Wars.

9
00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:03.120
[George] Beneath the car's topography lay hundreds of miles of interconnected limestone caverns, anchored by the world-famous Mammoth Cave.

10
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:15.040
[George] As automobile tourism exploded with the mass adoption of the Model T Ford, local landowners recognized that a spectacular subterranean cavern was more valuable than a dozen tobacco farms.

11
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:33.840
[George] Rival cave owners engaged in ruthless commercial competition, erecting billboard barricades, hiring roadside cappers to divert tourist motor cars, and secretly exploring sinkholes in search of new, undiscovered wonders with grand stalactites and crystal chambers that could outshine Mammoth Cave.

12
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:41.600
[George] Among the most daring, skilled, and obsessive native cavers in the region was 37-year-old William Floyd Collins.

13
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:51.840
[George] Since childhood, Floyd had spent weeks underground alone with a single kerosene lantern, wriggling through tight limestone fissures where no human had ever set foot.

14
00:01:51.840 --> 00:02:01.880
[George] In 1917, Floyd had discovered Crystal Cave on his family's farm, but it was located along an isolated, muddy road that tourists rarely reached.

15
00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:09.400
[George] Floyd dreamed of finding a grand commercial cavern close to the main highway that would bring prosperity to his aging parents.

16
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:20.520
[George] Kentucky cave owners engaged in bitter midnight turf wars, posting armed guards at cavern entrances and slashing automobile tires of tourists heading toward competing properties.

17
00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:32.280
[George] The dramatic events unfolded against a backdrop of deep cultural transformation, as rural communities confronted the rapid onset of modern industrial technology and national media attention.

18
00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:46.360
[George] Floyd knew the labyrinthine subterranean passages of Barron County better than any man in Kentucky, having spent his youth crawling through tight limestone fissures with a handheld kerosene lantern searching for hidden geological wonders.

19
00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:59.880
[George] On the freezing morning of Friday, January 30, 1925, Floyd Collins struck an agreement with three neighboring farmers to explore a promising sinkhole near the cave city turnpike known as Sand Cave.

20
00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:06.760
[George] Armed with a single kerosene lamp and wearing a denim jacket, Floyd entered the narrow sandstone crevice.

21
00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:15.720
[George] He squeezed on his stomach through tight, twisting fissures, descending 60 feet below the surface and traveling 150 feet into the earth.

22
00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:21.560
[George] At the bottom of a steep, slick chute, Floyd located a promising limestone chamber.

23
00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:31.480
[George] But on his return journey, as he wriggled upward through a constricted, 70-degree chimney barely larger than a coffin, his foot brushed against a protruding stone.

24
00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:39.560
[George] A twenty-seven-pound limestone boulder dislodged from the ceiling, pinning his left ankle against the rock floor in a natural vice.

25
00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:48.320
[George] In the struggle to free his foot, Floyd knocked over his kerosene lantern, plunging the narrow passage into absolute, suffocating blackness.

26
00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:58.560
[George] Water dripped relentlessly from the ceiling into the freezing mud, and Floyd realized he was trapped sixty feet beneath the surface, completely immobile in the icy dark.

27
00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:10.320
[George] Floyd had spent his entire life exploring the labyrinthine darkness beneath the surface, possessing an uncanny instinct for navigating subterranean rivers and delicate gypsum flower rooms.

28
00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:21.280
[George] Eyewitness accounts recorded in local diaries and regional newspapers captured the tension and uncertainty of the moment, preserving vivid details for future generations of historians.

29
00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:34.560
[George] The bitter February winter wind howled across the snow-covered Kentucky hills, while down in the narrow crawlway, the temperature hovered at a bone-chilling fifty degrees as cold water dripped steadily onto Floyd's pinned legs.

30
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:43.040
[George] Floyd failed to return that evening, and on Saturday morning, local neighbors crawled into the fissure, hearing his faint cries for help.

31
00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:49.520
[George] But the passage was so agonizingly narrow that grown men suffered acute panic attacks and could not reach him.

32
00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:58.240
[George] On Sunday, a slight, 21-year-old cub reporter for the Louisville Courier Journal named William Burke Skeets Miller arrived on the scene.

33
00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:05.760
[George] Because of his diminutive frame, weighing barely 115 pounds, Miller volunteered to crawl down the icy tunnel.

34
00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:14.720
[George] Pushing an electric light bulb ahead of him on a rubber wire, Miller squirmed through the claustrophobic squeeze on his stomach, reaching Floyd's side.

35
00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:24.400
[George] Miller fed the trapped caver coffee and sandwiches, held his hand in the freezing mud, and conducted an astonishing series of intimate underground interviews.

36
00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:31.080
[George] Miller's daily dispatches, syndicated by the Associated Press, electrified the American public.

37
00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:37.720
[George] Readers wept over Floyd's whispered prayers and Miller's vivid, harrowing descriptions of the cold stone chamber.

38
00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:43.000
[George] For his selfless courage, Skeets Miller was later awarded the Pulitzer Prize.

39
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:53.000
[George] Miller spent hours lying on his stomach in the freezing slurry, holding Floyd cold hands and singing hymns together to sustain the trapped explorer spirits in the pitch black.

40
00:05:53.000 --> 00:06:04.520
[George] William Miller was small enough to squeeze through the tight pinch points, braving falling rocks and wet clay to feed Collins' coffee and sandwiches while speaking gentle words of comfort into the gloom.

41
00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.760
[George] Within days, Sand Cave became the center of global attention.

42
00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:16.920
[George] Over 50,000 spectators, reporters, carnival vendors, and National Guardsmen descended upon the rural Kentucky hillside.

43
00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:21.400
[George] Special excursion trains brought crowds from Chicago and Cincinnati.

44
00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:26.200
[George] The scene on the surface transformed into a bizarre, grotesque circus.

45
00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:32.040
[George] Concessionaires sold barbecue, soda water, and commemorative Floyd Collins penance.

46
00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:39.320
[George] Patent medicine salesmen peddled miracle tonics, and jugglers performed for crowds gathered around the cave entrance.

47
00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:47.800
[George] Overhead, airplanes chartered by newsreel companies circled the winter sky, dropping packages of equipment and photographic film.

48
00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:54.520
[George] It was the third greatest media story of the interwar era, trailing only the Lindbergh flight and the armistice.

49
00:06:54.520 --> 00:07:10.520
[George] Preachers delivered fiery sermons on the hillside, warning that Floyd's entrapment was a divine judgment on human vanity, while radio networks broadcast live daily updates from the mouth of the fissure to millions of families gathered around their living room receivers.

50
00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:20.640
[George] Telegraph wires strung from the cave mouth carried hourly bulletins to radio stations across the nation, uniting millions of listeners in a shared vigil of hope and prayer.

51
00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:33.760
[George] Carnival barkers sold hot dogs, balloons and bootleg liquor from the beds of farm trucks, while local preachers delivered thunderous sermons on repentance to crowds of rubberneckers standing around the cave entrance.

52
00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:39.600
[George] As the media circus swirled above, the situation underground deteriorated rapidly.

53
00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:51.440
[George] Rescuers attempted to pull Floyd free using ropes attached to a harness, but the intense traction against his pinned ankle threatened to tear his leg from his body, causing Floyd agonizing pain.

54
00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.680
[George] Then, on Wednesday, February 4, disaster struck.

55
00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:05.360
[George] The warmth of dozens of human bodies crawling through the damp passage, combined with electric light bulbs, melted the permafrost holding the ceiling gravel together.

56
00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:13.520
[George] A massive section of the limestone tunnel collapsed behind Floyd, sealing the narrow crawlway with dozens of tons of boulders and mud.

57
00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:15.840
[George] Rescuers were completely cut off.

58
00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:20.800
[George] They could no longer reach Floyd with warm food, coffee or words of comfort.

59
00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:30.320
[George] A rubber hose was threaded through the debris to pump warm air into the chamber, but tapping signals on the limestone walls grew fainter with every passing hour.

60
00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:40.880
[George] Rescue engineers worked with mining lanterns clamped in their teeth, weeping openly as tons of wet limestone collapsed across the timber shoring, sealing the passage.

61
00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:53.840
[George] Miners from across the Appalachian coalfields volunteered their labor without pay, digging through solid limestone and fractured shale around the clock with pickaxes and shovels under the glare of temporary carbon arc lamps.

62
00:08:53.840 --> 00:09:09.000
[George] With the horizontal crawlway blocked, a desperate council of military engineers, coal mining experts, and state officials decided that the only remaining hope was to sink a vertical rescue shaft 60 feet through solid rock and clay from the surface.

63
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:22.440
[George] A crew of 75 volunteer miners twalled round the clock in freezing rain, hacking through sandstone and flint with hand picks and shovels, refusing to use dynamite for fear of collapsing Floyd's underground ceiling.

64
00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:25.640
[George] It was a race against hypothermia and starvation.

65
00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:34.360
[George] On Monday, February 16, after twelve grueling days of excavation, the miners finally broke through the rock into Floyd's chamber.

66
00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:36.760
[George] Skeets Miller and medical examiner Dr.

67
00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:39.240
[George] William Hazlitt climbed through the opening.

68
00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:42.840
[George] The electric light illuminated Floyd's motionless form.

69
00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:49.000
[George] He had succumbed to exposure, starvation, and lack of water and estimated three days earlier.

70
00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:55.000
[George] Floyd Collins was dead at 37, his left ankle still held tight by the fatal boulder.

71
00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:05.720
[George] The miners twalled through sleet and freezing rain for twelve sleepless days, driven by an unyielding brotherhood that refused to surrender hope until the final rock was cleared.

72
00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:17.800
[George] The courage and resilience demonstrated by everyday citizens during this critical trial inspired observers across the country, showing that ordinary people can rise to meet extraordinary challenges.

73
00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:29.480
[George] The grim announcement hushed the carnival atmosphere on the hillside, sending a wave of sorrow through the crowd as reporters hurried to telegraph offices to transmit the news of Floyd's tragic demise.

74
00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:33.800
[George] Even in death, Floyd Collins could not escape the spectacle.

75
00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:47.920
[George] In 1927, his family sold Crystal Cave and Floyd's embalmed body to a commercial promoter, who placed his remains inside a glass-topped casket inside the cavern, charging tourists to view the legendary explorer.

76
00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:56.080
[George] In 1929, body thieves stole his corpse, which was recovered in a nearby field minus his injured left leg.

77
00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:05.360
[George] Finally, in 1989, following decades of federal litigation, the National Park Service granted Floyd's surviving relatives their deepest wish.

78
00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:14.160
[George] His body was removed from the cave and given a sacred, Christian burial in the cemetery of the Mammoth Cave Baptist Church beneath the Kentucky Pines.

79
00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:19.200
[George] Floyd Collins's tragic ordeal transformed cave exploration forever.

80
00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:33.760
[George] Inspiring modern subterranean rescue standards and catalyzing the federal creation of Mammoth Cave National Park, an immortal testament to a pioneer explorer whose love for the dark wonders of the earth captured the soul of an entire nation.

81
00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:48.240
[George] The tragedy of Sand Cave transformed speleology into an organized scientific discipline and inspired the creation of the National Speleological Society, ensuring that Floyd's sacrifice would protect future subterranean explorers.

82
00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:59.920
[George] Today, historic markers and museum collections honor these legendary figures, reminding us that the spirit of American adventure and perseverance continues to guide our shared journey forward.

83
00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:14.480
[George] The tragic ordeal of Floyd Collins captured the imagination of a nation during the dawn of mass media, inspiring books, folk songs, and the establishment of Mammoth Cave National Park as a protected subterranean wilderness.

84
00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:23.120
[Rebecca] Floyd Collins's Stand Beneath the Limestone hills reminds us of the fragile boundary between human courage and nature's unyielding power.

85
00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:24.400
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

86
00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:32.360
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey to Dayton, Tennessee, for the intellectual trial of the century the Scopes Monkey Trial.

